---
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import { invoiceRows } from '../../lib/fixtures';
import ClassRef from '../../components/ClassRef.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
import ApiTable from '../../components/ApiTable.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const poAppHref = 'https://github.com/Busy-Office/busy-office-ui/tree/main/examples/po-app';
const tableMarkup = `<!-- tabindex="0": the container scrolls when the table
     overflows, and a scrollable region must be keyboard-reachable -->
<div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
  <div class="bo-data-table__toolbar">
    <div class="bo-data-table__bulk-actions" role="group" aria-label="Bulk actions">
      <button class="bo-btn bo-btn--secondary" type="button">Approve</button>
    </div>
    <span class="bo-data-table__selection-count"></span> <!-- live "n selected" -->
    <input class="bo-input" type="search" aria-label="Filter invoices" />
  </div>
  <table class="bo-data-table">
    <thead><tr>
      <th scope="col"><input type="checkbox"
          class="bo-checkbox bo-data-table__select-all" aria-label="Select all" /></th>
      <th scope="col" aria-sort="ascending">
        <button class="bo-data-table__sort-btn" type="button">Invoice #</button>
      </th>
      <th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Amount</th>
    </tr></thead>
    <tbody><tr>
      <td><input type="checkbox" name="id" value="INV-1"
          class="bo-checkbox bo-data-table__row-select" aria-label="Select INV-1" /></td>
      <!-- name/value make the selection POSTable. Prefer a real <form> around
           the rows AND the bulk buttons over hx-include on the button: both
           send the same ids, but only the form gives you Enter-from-any-row
           (native implicit submission). -->
      <td class="bo-data-table__col--code">INV-1</td>
      <td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">$4,208.00</td>
    </tr></tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<script type="module">
  import { initDataTables } from "@busy-office/ui/js";
  initDataTables(); // select-all, live count, O(1) toolbar reveal
</script>
<!-- Sorting contract: YOUR code (server or client) re-orders rows and sets
     aria-sort on the <th>; the CSS draws the indicator from aria-sort. -->`;
const rows = invoiceRows;

/* 50 columns x 10 rows — the WIDE counterpart to the rows-at-scale section
   below it. Generated rather than typed so the demo cannot drift out of the
   shape it claims to test (roadmap 30.3). */
const wideCols = [
  'Item', 'Description', 'Vendor', 'Vendor part', 'UoM', 'Qty', 'Unit price', 'Currency',
  'Line total', 'Tax code', 'Tax %', 'Discount %', 'Net', 'Cost center', 'GL account',
  'Project', 'WBS', 'Plant', 'Storage loc', 'Warehouse', 'Bin', 'Batch', 'Serial',
  'Lead time', 'Requested', 'Confirmed', 'Delivered', 'Goods receipt', 'Invoice',
  'Payment terms', 'Incoterm', 'Ship-to', 'Bill-to', 'Buyer', 'Requester', 'Approver',
  'Approved on', 'Status', 'Blocked', 'Reason', 'Contract', 'Agreement', 'Price list',
  'Condition', 'Rebate', 'Freight', 'Duty', 'Origin', 'HS code', 'Last changed',
];
const wideRows = Array.from({ length: 10 }, (_, r) =>
  wideCols.map((c, i) => (i === 0 ? `LINE-${String(r + 1).padStart(3, '0')}` : `${c.slice(0, 3).toUpperCase()}-${r + 1}${i}`)));
---
<Gallery title="Data table">
  <p class="demo-note">
    Check a row — the bulk-action toolbar appears via <code>:has()</code>, zero JS.
    Sticky header, tabular numerals, density-aware row heights, container-query
    auto-compaction below 480px container width. Two tiers, same markup family:
    <strong>Simple</strong> (below) covers selection/sort/filter for free; reach for
    <strong>Advanced</strong> (<code>data-grid-nav</code> + <code>initDataGrid()</code>,
    further down this page) only when a screen genuinely needs full two-axis
    keyboard navigation — most tables don't.
  </p>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Simple — select, sort, filter</h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <div class="bo-data-table__toolbar">
        <div class="bo-data-table__bulk-actions" role="group" aria-label="Bulk actions">
          <button class="bo-btn bo-btn--secondary" type="button">Approve</button>
          <button class="bo-btn bo-btn--danger" type="button">Reject</button>
        </div>
        <span class="bo-data-table__selection-count"></span>
        <input class="bo-input" type="search" aria-label="Filter invoices" placeholder="Filter invoices…" style="max-inline-size: 16rem" />
      </div>
      <table class="bo-data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col"><input type="checkbox" class="bo-checkbox bo-data-table__select-all" aria-label="Select all" /></th>
            <th scope="col" aria-sort="ascending">
              <button class="bo-data-table__sort-btn" type="button">Invoice #</button>
            </th>
            <th scope="col">Vendor</th>
            <th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--secondary">Cost center</th>
            <th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Amount</th>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td><input type="checkbox" class="bo-checkbox bo-data-table__row-select" aria-label={`Select ${r.no}`} /></td>
              <td class="bo-data-table__col--code">{r.no}</td>
              <td>{r.vendor}</td>
              <td class="bo-data-table__col--secondary bo-data-table__col--code">{r.cc}</td>
              <td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">{r.amount}</td>
              <td><span class={`bo-badge bo-badge--${r.tone}`}>{r.status}</span></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Narrow container → auto-compaction</h2>
    <div style="max-inline-size: 420px">
      <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
        <table class="bo-data-table bo-data-table--striped">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Invoice #</th>
              <th scope="col">Vendor</th>
              <th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--secondary">Cost center</th>
              <th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Amount</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {rows.slice(0, 3).map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="bo-data-table__col--code">{r.no}</td>
                <td>{r.vendor}</td>
                <td class="bo-data-table__col--secondary">{r.cc}</td>
                <td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">{r.amount}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">The cost-center column hides itself; rows tighten — driven by the container's width, not the viewport.
    <strong>Reflow requirement (WCAG 1.4.10):</strong> data hidden by compaction must stay
    reachable — give rows a detail view (like the PO # link pattern) or don't mark
    columns <code>--secondary</code>.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Editing, toolbars and grid navigation</h2>
    <p>Two opt-in layers live on their own pages so this one stays the
    table itself: <a href={base + '/components/inline-editing'}>inline
    editing</a> (seamless cell inputs, dirty state, Save/Cancel and the
    events you persist with) and
    <a href={base + '/components/table-toolbar'}>toolbar, columns &amp;
    grid navigation</a> (column visibility, export, and the roving
    keyboard grid model).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>The assembled screen lives in Patterns</h2>
    <p>Toolbar + sticky column + footer pagination + filter bar composed
    into a real invoice list — with its anatomy, data contract, states,
    and keyboard walkthrough — is
    <a href={base + '/patterns/invoice-list'}>the invoice-list pattern</a>.
    This page documents the table itself; the pattern documents the
    screen.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Loading & error rows</h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-loading="true" aria-busy="true">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Invoice #</th><th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Amount</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">INV-10234</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">$4,208.00</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">INV-10235</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">$18,940.50</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><code>data-loading="true"</code> (or
    <code>aria-busy</code>) dims the table and blocks interaction mid-swap — flip it
    from your fetch/HTMX code. For first-load, prefer
    <a href={base + '/components/state-patterns'}>skeleton rows</a>.</p>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0" style="margin-block-start: var(--bo-space-4)">
      <table class="bo-data-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Invoice #</th><th scope="col">Problem</th><th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Amount</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">INV-10240</td><td>—</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">$1,120.00</td></tr>
          <tr data-row-state="error"><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">INV-10241</td><td><span class="bo-badge bo-badge--danger">Duplicate</span> already posted as INV-10198</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">$5,300.00</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">INV-10242</td><td>—</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">$980.00</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><code>data-row-state="error"</code> — the tint plus a
    start border, and the badge/message in the row carries the reason (two-channel:
    never the color alone). This is the visual channel the dirty row state
    above reuses in amber.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Performance at scale — measured, not guessed</h2>
    <p>Measured 2026-08-15 against the full table stack (sticky header, row
    checkboxes, badges, container query, <code>initDataTables()</code>) in the
    <a href={poAppHref}><code>examples/po-app</code></a> stress harness (<code>/stress?n=…</code>):</p>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Rows</th><th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Initial render</th><th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Select-all</th><th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Post-bulk-check style flush</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">1,000</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">85 ms</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">4 ms</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">negligible</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">5,000</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">174 ms</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">18 ms</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">231 ms</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">20,000</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">558 ms</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">49 ms</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">610 ms</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">5,000 <span class="bo-badge">4× CPU throttle</span></td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">1,625 ms</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">15 ms</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">174 ms</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">20,000 <span class="bo-badge">4× CPU throttle</span></td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">3,783 ms</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">60 ms</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">686 ms</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">The 4×-throttle rows approximate older corporate
    hardware (added after the decisions grill challenged the single-machine
    evidence). Render and scroll scale linearly with no cliff; the recurring cost
    is the <em>bulk-select style recalculation</em> (the selected-row tint
    re-evaluating per row) — it repeats on EVERY bulk toggle, staying under 700 ms
    even throttled. The genuine pain point is initial render at 20k rows on slow
    hardware (~3.8 s). Practical guidance: a few thousand rows are fine as-is;
    beyond that, paginate server-side (<a href={base + '/components/pagination'}>page
    numbers or load-more</a>) — which dense ERP screens want anyway for
    findability. <strong>There are no virtualization hooks</strong>: windowing
    breaks Ctrl-F, select-all semantics, and screen-reader row counts, and
    pagination is the answer at the scale where tables stop being scannable.
    Re-open condition, not dogma: if a real deployment measures interaction costs
    breaching ~1 s at row counts it genuinely cannot paginate, the
    <code>/stress</code> harness in <a href={poAppHref}><code>examples/po-app</code></a> is kept for
    exactly that re-measurement.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Wide tables — 50 columns</h2>
    <p>The counterpart to the row counts above: an ERP line table that grew
    sideways. Ten rows, <strong>fifty columns</strong>, first column frozen with
    <code>.bo-data-table--sticky-col</code>. Scroll it horizontally — the Item
    column stays put, the header stays put, and both stay opaque over the cells
    passing underneath.</p>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0" aria-label="Fifty-column purchase order lines, scrollable">
      <table class="bo-data-table bo-data-table--sticky-col" data-density="compact">
        <caption class="bo-visually-hidden">Purchase order lines, 50 columns</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>{wideCols.map((c, i) => (
            <th scope="col" class={i >= 5 && i <= 12 ? 'bo-data-table__col--numeric' : undefined}>{c}</th>
          ))}</tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {wideRows.map((row) => (
            <tr>{row.map((cell, i) => (
              i === 0
                ? <th scope="row">{cell}</th>
                : <td class={i >= 5 && i <= 12 ? 'bo-data-table__col--numeric' : undefined}>{cell}</td>
            ))}</tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">
      The scroll container is the focusable element (<code>tabindex="0"</code> with an
      <code>aria-label</code>), so a keyboard user is not stranded behind fifty tab stops:
      focus the region and the arrow keys scroll it. <strong>Measured, because "reachable"
      is not the same as "usable":</strong> each press moves about 40px, so the fiftieth
      column is roughly <strong>76 presses</strong> away, and <kbd class="bo-kbd">End</kbd>
      does not help — this container scrolls horizontally only, and End acts on the
      vertical axis. Reachable, then, but tedious by any honest reading, which is the next
      argument for the column chooser below rather than a defect to paper over.
    </p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">
      <strong>Fifty visible columns is usually a symptom, and this page will say so
      rather than just prove it renders.</strong> Nobody reads fifty columns; they read
      the twelve their job needs. The ERP answer is a <em>column chooser</em> plus
      <a href={base + '/patterns/invoice-list'}>saved views</a> — let each role pick its
      columns once and store the choice, so Purchasing sees prices and the warehouse sees
      bins. Ship the wide table as the <em>source</em> of those views, never as the
      default screen. If you genuinely need all fifty at once, that is a report or an
      export, not a screen — see <a href={base + '/patterns/staging'}>the Excel
      round-trip</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Multiple frozen columns</h2>
    <p><code>--sticky-col</code> above freezes ONE column for free — its offset
    is always 0, no JS needed. Freezing a <strong>second or third</strong> column
    needs each earlier column's actual rendered width, which nothing knows in
    advance (columns size to content by default) — so this is the one case in
    this page's table story that opts into JS: <code>data-sticky-cols="2"</code>
    plus <code>initStickyCols()</code>, which measures the header row and keeps
    the offsets live across resizes. Capped at 3 columns — freezing more than
    that is usually a sign the table needs a column chooser, not more frozen
    columns.</p>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0" aria-label="Fifty-column purchase order lines, two columns frozen">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-sticky-cols="2" data-density="compact">
        <caption class="bo-visually-hidden">Purchase order lines, first two columns frozen</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>{wideCols.map((c, i) => (
            <th scope="col" class={i >= 5 && i <= 12 ? 'bo-data-table__col--numeric' : undefined}>{c}</th>
          ))}</tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {wideRows.map((row) => (
            <tr>{row.map((cell, i) => (
              i === 0
                ? <th scope="row">{cell}</th>
                : <td class={i >= 5 && i <= 12 ? 'bo-data-table__col--numeric' : undefined}>{cell}</td>
            ))}</tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Scroll horizontally — Line and the second column
    both stay put now, not just the first. Both frozen columns and the header
    stay opaque over the cells passing underneath, same as the single-column
    case.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Right-click column menu</h2>
    <p><code>data-context-menu</code> opens the SAME popover menu component
    <code>initDropdowns()</code> already renders for the toolbar's Columns
    picker — right-click a header instead of clicking a button, and the menu
    opens at the cursor. This behavior (<code>initContextMenu()</code>) owns
    exactly one thing: positioning. It doesn't own sorting or hiding — those
    menu items below call the exact mechanisms this page already documents
    (<code>aria-sort</code> for sort, <code>data-col-toggle</code> for hide),
    the same as clicking the existing sort button or toolbar checkbox would.
    A per-column filter item and a runtime "always visible" pin were graded
    out of scope for the framework — see <code>.roundtable/</code> for the
    reasoning.</p>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table">
        <thead><tr>
          <th scope="col" data-context-menu="col-menu-vendor" aria-sort="none">
            <button class="bo-data-table__sort-btn" type="button">Vendor</button>
          </th>
          <th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric" data-context-menu="col-menu-amount" aria-sort="none">
            <button class="bo-data-table__sort-btn" type="button">Amount</button>
          </th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td data-col="vendor">Acme Supply Co.</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric" data-col="amount">$4,208.00</td></tr>
          <tr><td data-col="vendor">Globex Industrial</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric" data-col="amount">$18,940.50</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="bo-dropdown__menu" id="col-menu-vendor" popover>
        <button class="bo-dropdown__item" type="button">Sort ascending</button>
        <button class="bo-dropdown__item" type="button">Sort descending</button>
        <hr class="bo-dropdown__separator" />
        <label class="bo-dropdown__item"><input type="checkbox" class="bo-checkbox" data-col-toggle="vendor" checked /> Show column</label>
      </div>
      <div class="bo-dropdown__menu" id="col-menu-amount" popover>
        <button class="bo-dropdown__item" type="button">Sort ascending</button>
        <button class="bo-dropdown__item" type="button">Sort descending</button>
        <hr class="bo-dropdown__separator" />
        <label class="bo-dropdown__item"><input type="checkbox" class="bo-checkbox" data-col-toggle="amount" checked /> Show column</label>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Right-click "Vendor" or "Amount" above. The Sort
    items are static here, the same way the "Simple" section's sort button at
    the top of this page is — wiring them to actually re-order rows is
    consumer code either way, on-page click or context-menu click makes no
    difference to that contract. "Show column" is real: uncheck it and the
    column disappears immediately, because it's the exact same
    <code>data-col-toggle</code> checkbox the toolbar pattern uses, just
    rendered inside a right-click menu instead of a toolbar dropdown — the
    menu's own markup has to live INSIDE the same
    <code>.bo-data-table-container</code> as the table for that to work
    (<code>data-col-toggle</code>'s hide logic looks up its container via
    <code>.closest()</code>; a popover placed as a sibling after the
    container, which is where it would naturally land if authored after the
    closing <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> tag, silently finds no container and
    does nothing — caught live on this exact demo before it shipped).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Grouped rows + subtotals</h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table">
        <thead><tr>
          <th scope="col">PO #</th>
          <th scope="col">Vendor</th>
          <th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Amount</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="rowgroup" colspan="3">CC-1180 — Facilities</th></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">PO-88212</td><td>Initech GmbH</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">$730.25</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">PO-88214</td><td>Stark Components</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">$56,000.00</td></tr>
          <tr><td colspan="2" class="bo-data-table__col--right">Subtotal CC-1180</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">$56,730.25</td></tr>
        </tbody>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="rowgroup" colspan="3">CC-4021 — Warehouse</th></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">PO-88210</td><td>Acme Supply Co.</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">$4,208.00</td></tr>
          <tr><td colspan="2" class="bo-data-table__col--right">Subtotal CC-4021</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">$4,208.00</td></tr>
        </tbody>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td colspan="2" class="bo-data-table__col--right"><strong>Grand total</strong></td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric"><strong>$60,938.25</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">No grouping component exists because none is needed —
    this composes from plain HTML table semantics the CSS already styles: one
    <code>&lt;tbody&gt;</code> per group, the group header a
    <code>&lt;th scope="rowgroup" colspan&gt;</code> row (bold by default; the
    <code>scope</code> declares it heads its <code>&lt;tbody&gt;</code> — screen-reader
    support for rowgroup association varies, so keep group names ALSO in prose/
    captions when the grouping is load-bearing), subtotal and grand-total rows are ordinary
    rows using <code>__col--right</code>/<code>__col--numeric</code>. Proven in the
    <a href={poAppHref}><code>examples/po-app</code></a> "Spend by cost center" screen, both themes.
    Don't combine with <code>--striped</code> (stripes land arbitrarily on summary
    rows) or with row-select/sort (sorting across grouped bodies is your code's
    problem — regroup server-side instead).</p>
  </section>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Conditional cell tone</h2>
    <p><code>data-tone="danger|warning|success"</code> on a <code>&lt;td&gt;</code>
    tints that one cell — subtle background plus an inset accent, same shape
    as row state above, scoped to a single cell instead of a whole row. The
    framework never decides <em>why</em> a cell is toned: a negative balance,
    a budget threshold, any business rule — that condition is computed
    server-side and rendered as this one attribute. Pair the tone with text
    that says the same thing (a sign, a word, a badge) — the color is not the
    only channel. Background is the default; add <code>data-tone-text</code>
    to also tint the text (the CC-1180 row below) — opt-in rather than
    automatic, since forcing text color on every toned cell would fight a
    value that's already bold or already colored by something else.</p>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table">
        <thead><tr>
          <th scope="col">Cost centre</th>
          <th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Budget</th>
          <th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Balance</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>CC-1180 — Facilities</td>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">$60,000.00</td>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric" data-tone="danger" data-tone-text>−$730.25</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>CC-4021 — Warehouse</td>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">$5,000.00</td>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric" data-tone="warning">$4,780.00</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>CC-2200 — IT</td>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">$12,000.00</td>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric" data-tone="success">$3,120.00</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">The minus sign on the first Balance cell carries
    the "over budget" meaning in text, same as the badges elsewhere on this
    page carry status in text — <code>data-tone</code> only repeats it
    visually. Composes with row state: a cell's own <code>--bo-cell-bg</code>
    wins over its row's, so a toned cell inside a dirty or striped row still
    reads correctly.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Column alignment</h2>
    <p>Put the same modifier on the <code>&lt;th&gt;</code> and every <code>&lt;td&gt;</code>
    in a column: <code>--left</code>, <code>--center</code>, <code>--right</code>, or
    <code>--numeric</code> (right-aligned <em>and</em> tabular figures, for amounts).</p>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--left">Item (left)</th>
            <th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--center">Status (center)</th>
            <th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--right">Weight (right)</th>
            <th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Amount (numeric)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--left">Standing desk</td>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--center"><span class="bo-badge bo-badge--success">Approved</span></td>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--right">12 kg</td>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">1,208.00</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--left">Monitor arm</td>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--center"><span class="bo-badge bo-badge--warning">Pending</span></td>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--right">3 kg</td>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">92.50</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Alignment is a single class per cell and wins over the
    default (start) reliably — headers and body cells align together.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Column width &amp; long content</h2>
    <p>Columns already size themselves to their content — the table never sets
    <code>table-layout: fixed</code>, so the browser's default column-width
    algorithm does the "fit the contents" work with zero CSS from this
    framework. What that algorithm doesn't do on its own is stop a column
    growing forever, and the fix has a real trap in it:
    <code>max-inline-size</code> on the <code>&lt;td&gt;</code> itself is
    silently ignored under <code>table-layout: auto</code> unless the row is
    already under width pressure — a table with room to spare just renders the
    cell at its natural width, max-width or not. Put the cap on an inner
    element instead (a <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> is enough) alongside the
    existing <code>.bo-u-text-truncate</code> utility — plain CSS, not a
    data-table-specific mechanism.</p>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table">
        <thead><tr>
          <th scope="col">PO #</th>
          <th scope="col">Vendor</th>
          <th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Amount</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--code">PO-88214</td>
            <td><span class="bo-u-text-truncate" style="display: block; max-inline-size: 10rem">Stark Components International Holdings</span></td>
            <td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">$56,000.00</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">The full vendor name is still in the DOM — this
    is a visual clip, not a data loss, so a screen reader and <kbd class="bo-kbd">Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd class="bo-kbd">F</kbd>
    still see the whole string. Give the inner element a <code>title</code>
    attribute if the clipped tail matters enough to show on hover.
    <a href={poAppHref}><code>examples/po-app</code></a>'s own Vendor column
    uses the bare <code>&lt;td&gt;</code> form without a fixed cap — that only
    truncates once the whole table is genuinely squeezed for space, which is a
    different, weaker guarantee than this section's deliberate cap; worth
    fixing there too, tracked as a follow-up rather than folded in silently
    here.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>Font styling</strong> (bold, emphasis) is
    the same story: no utility class exists or is planned for it — a subtotal
    or grand-total row uses a real <code>&lt;strong&gt;</code> (see "Grouped
    rows + subtotals" above), which is both simpler and correct HTML semantics
    a screen reader can act on. Adding a <code>bo-u-font-bold</code>-style
    class would be a second way to do something native HTML already does —
    this framework ships "semantic components, not utility soup" on purpose.</p>
  </section>
  <ClassRef component="data-table" />
  <ApiTable
    component="data-table"
    js="Optional but recommended: initDataTables() — select-all, live count, O(1) toolbar reveal, swap re-derive. Without it: selection visuals work via :has(); select-all is inert."
    notes={[
      'Sorting contract: the framework paints the indicator from <code>aria-sort</code> on the <code>&lt;th&gt;</code>; re-ordering rows and updating the attribute is YOUR code (server or client).',
      'Row checkboxes need <code>name</code>/<code>value</code> to POST selections. Wrap the rows and the bulk buttons in one <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> rather than reaching for <code>hx-include</code>: only the form gives native implicit submission, so <kbd>Enter</kbd> from any row runs the bulk action instead of Shift-Tabbing back to a toolbar above the table. Make only the SAFE action <code>type="submit"</code> — implicit submission fires the first submit button, and that must never be a destructive one.',
      'If the bulk swap replaces the rows, give each row checkbox an <code>id</code>: htmx restores focus after a swap only to a matching id, and without it the keyboard user lands on <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>.',
      'Columns marked <code>__col--secondary</code> hide under container compaction — keep that data reachable (row detail view) per WCAG 1.4.10.',
      'Separately opt-in: <code>data-grid-nav</code> + <code>initDataGrid()</code> for the full WAI-ARIA APG Grid pattern (two-axis keyboard navigation). Off by default — a read-only reference table does not need per-cell Tab stops.',
      'Separately opt-in: <code>data-row-edit</code> + <code>initRowEdit()</code> for multi-row inline edit with per-row dirty state and Save/Cancel. Tracks state only — persistence is your code, via the <code>bo:row-save</code> event.',
      'Separately opt-in: <code>data-col-toggle</code>/<code>data-table-export</code> + <code>initTableToolbar()</code> for column visibility (composes with the multi-select dropdown, no new markup) and an export intent (<code>bo:table-export</code> — generating the file is your code). Rows appended later (load-more, swaps) don\'t inherit hidden columns — call <code>initTableToolbar()</code> again after appending, same re-call convention as <code>initDataTables()</code>.',
      '<code>data-tone="danger|warning|success"</code> on a <code>&lt;td&gt;</code> tints that cell (CSS-only, no JS). The framework paints the tone; deciding which cells get one — a negative balance, a threshold, any rule — is server-side logic, never the framework\'s. Add <code>data-tone-text</code> alongside it to also tint the text (opt-in, not automatic).',
      'Separately opt-in: <code>data-sticky-cols="1|2|3"</code> + <code>initStickyCols()</code> freezes that many leading columns. One column needs no JS (<code>--sticky-col</code>, offset always 0); two or three need JS because column widths aren\'t known until rendered — the behavior measures the header row and keeps offsets live via <code>ResizeObserver</code>.',
      'Separately opt-in: <code>data-context-menu="menu-id"</code> + <code>initContextMenu()</code> opens a <code>.bo-dropdown__menu</code> at the cursor on right-click instead of at a button. It only positions the menu — the items inside call whatever this page already documents (an <code>aria-sort</code> control, a <code>data-col-toggle</code> checkbox); this behavior owns no sort or filter logic of its own.',
    ]}
